#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079683 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n\n$$\n{3x^{2} + kx,\\begin{vmatrix}x < - 3, \\\\ x^{3},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge - 3\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a jump discontinuity at $x=-3$. However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the jump discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the left and right-sided limits of the function to be equal at $x=-3$:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nFor the left limit, we have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-3)^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → (-3)^{-})(3x^{2} + kx) \\\\\n&= 3(-3)^{2} + k(-3) \\\\\n&= 27 - 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-3)^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → (-3)^{+})x^{3} \\\\\n&= (-3)^{3} \\\\\n&=-27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the left and right limits as equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n27 - 3k &= -27 \\\\\n3k &= 54 \\\\\nk &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-3$, we need to choose $k=18$." :tutorial/id #uuid "10dabe2c-998a-5731-bcf2-c0ed93bc7f2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 543 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
